 ## Overview
-Threaded Chunk Rendering is a parallel execution strategy that distributes volumetric rendering tasks across a pool of worker threads to maximize hardware utilization. It solves the problem of CPU-bound rendering bottlenecks by decoupling task scheduling from execution, allowing multiple chunks of the world to be rendered concurrently. It differs from [CPU Multithreaded Rendering](6052-cpu-multithreaded-rendering.md) by explicitly managing a queue of reusable renderers and synchronization primitives to handle dynamic workloads, and from [CPU-Driven Task Spawning](4397-cpu-driven-task-spawning.md) by using a fixed-size thread pool rather than spawning new threads per task.
+Threaded Chunk Rendering is a CPU-side parallelization strategy that distributes volumetric rendering tasks across a fixed-size thread pool to maximize multi-core utilization while managing synchronization overhead [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [CPU Multithreaded Rendering](6052-cpu-multithreaded-rendering.md) by explicitly decoupling task generation from execution via a queue-based manager, and from [Multi-GPU and Cluster Rendering](10538-multi-gpu-and-cluster-rendering.md) by remaining strictly within a single process and CPU core set [S1, unofficial]. The method solves the problem of dynamic load balancing in chunk-based voxel worlds by reusing renderer instances rather than spawning threads per frame [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A 3D grid of world chunks defined by dimensions $LOADED\_WORLD\_SIZE \times LOADED\_WORLD\_SIZE \times LOADED\_WORLD\_SIZE$, where each chunk has a fixed physical size (e.g., $8 \times 8 \times 8$ blocks) [S1, unofficial].
-**Outputs:** A composited image frame, where Z-buffering is used to resolve depth conflicts between concurrently rendered chunks [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+- Input: A grid of loaded world chunks (dimensions $0 \le i, j, k < \text{LOADED\_WORLD\_SIZE}$) and a pool of pre-allocated or lazily-created Renderer objects [S1, unofficial].
+- Output: Rendered frames for each chunk, composited into the final image (typically using Z-buffering to handle overdraw) [S1, unofficial].
 
